        "patient": "The patient presented with a mesenteric mass attributed to a desmoid tumor but had no mechanical or surgical complication, with no evidence of bowel occlusion, digestive perforation, or mesenteric ischemia. A histological diagnosis was obtained via percutaneous core-needle biopsy when anatomically possible, rather than relying on cytology. Molecular testing was performed as first-line evaluation to assess for a somatic pathogenic *CTNNB1* variant, and no pathogenic *CTNNB1* variant was detected. Given the negative *CTNNB1* result, constitutional testing for a pathogenic *APC* variant was performed and returned positive, consistent with APC-linked familial adenomatous polyposis. Even in the context of proven APC-linked FAP and expert-center imaging, biopsy was not considered potentially morbid in this case. There was strong clinical concern for an alternative diagnosis such as lymphoma or carcinoma in the setting of an FAP patient with a suggestive mesenteric mass, yet percutaneous biopsy was not feasible and endoscopic biopsy was not required because a percutaneous route had been achievable. The primary clinical focus was management.",
